    "interpretation": "DiskANN/Vamana (alpha-RNG diversification pruning, same family as HNSW/NSG) confirms the in-degree blow-up: max in-degree climbs with the duplicate sink. But Vamana collapses HARDER and as a CLIFF, not a gradient: its SINGLE medoid entry point is captured by the densest region (the sink) once duplication exceeds ~0.5%, so recall drops to ~0 and a larger Lsearch cannot rescue it (a trap, cf. the HNSW failure-mode analysis). The mega-hub pathology thus spans a THIRD real graph family; the in-degree marker generalizes while the exact failure geometry (entry-point capture vs. budget exhaustion) is index-specific. Contrast NNDescent (multi_index.json), whose in-degree barely moves."
